@group
    @title=HTML template
    @brief=The section describes the template that creates HTML based documentation from the source files
    @ingroup=templates
    @key=htmlTemplate

    The template is used to create the HTML documentation
    for WEB publishing as well as to be compiled using
    HTML Help Compiler.

    The template is located at
    `%docgen%/template/cs2ds/main.xsl`

    This template creates multiple output files, so use `file` parameter
    of the [clink=docProjectFile1.output]output[/clink] element of the project file
    to specify the destination folder:

    Example:

    ```xml
    <dg:output template="%docgen%/template/html/main.xsl" file="dst/null-file" encoding="utf-8">
    ```

    The template has the following parameters:

    @table
        @width=100%
        @row
            @header=yes
            @col
                @width=30%
                Parameter
            @end
            @col
                Description
            @end
        @end

        @row
            @col
                `text-language`
            @end
            @col
                2-letter language code, e.g. `en`.

                The parameter is used to localize the output
                documentation. `en`, `fr` and `ru` are supported.
            @end
        @end

        @row
            @col
                `help-title`
            @end
            @col
                The title of the help
            @end
        @end

        @row
            @col
                `write-advanced-web-content`
            @end
            @col
                `yes` or `no`

                The flag indicating whether the content tree
                must be interactive (e.g. you can collapse nodes)
            @end
        @end
        @row
            @col
                `default-transform`
            @end
            @col
                `yes` or `no`

                The flag indicating whether the compiler must
                escape HTML character and process bbcodes.

                The option is mostly used for backward
                compatibly with very old sources that
                has been created before bbcodes are introduced.
            @end
        @end
        @row
            @col
                `enable-highlighter`
            @end
            @col
                `yes` or `no`

                The flag indicating whether a syntax highlighting
                must be enabled for the samples.

                [url=https://highlightjs.org/]Highligh JS[/url]
                is used for highlight the syntax.
            @end
        @end
        @row
            @col
                `add-signature-to-brief`
            @end
            @col
                `yes` or `no`

                The flag indicating whether the parameters needs to be added
                to the name of the methods and to constructors in the member lists.
                The option is useful for libraries with a lot of overrides. See also
                [link=modelDS.class]`@class/@writeSignatures`[/link] property.
            @end
        @end
        @row
            @col
                `external-resources`
            @end
            @col
                `yes` or `no`

                If the value is `yes` the scripts and css
                are loaded from `res/scripts.js` and
                `res/styles.js` file.

                The default implementation would be copied
                during the compilation, but you can
                redefine the styles and scripts behavior.
            @end
        @end
        @row
            @col
                `add-custom-script`
            @end
            @col
                The relative file name or URL for a script to be added
            @end
        @end
        @row
            @col
                `add-custom-style`
            @end
            @col
                The relative file name or URL for a custom stylesheet to be added
            @end
        @end
        @row
            @col
                `write-hhp`
            @end
            @col
                `yes` or `no`, default is `yes`

                If value is "yes" an HHP project file will be created.

                You can call HTML Help Compiler 1.3 or 1.4 to create a CHM file.

                The flag indicating whether HTML Help project needs to be generated
            @end
        @end

        @row
            @col
                `hhp-language`
            @end
            @col
                The full name of the language, e.g. `0x409 English (United States)`

                The parameter is used to generate HTML Help project file
            @end
        @end

        @row
            @col
                `chm-file`
            @end
            @col
                The name of the CHM file to be created.

                The parameter is used to generate HTML Help project file
            @end
        @end
    @end
@end

@article
    @title=Adding Custom Content
    @brief=The article describes how to add custom content/make minor customizations
    @key=htmlCustomContent
    @ingroup=htmlTemplate

    #### Copying Content into Result

    The easiest way to add custom content or customize the content is simply add images, html files into
    output folder and then reference them from your content.

    For example, all you need to add the icon the documentation is to put
    `favicon.ico` file into the root of the compiled documentation.

    #### Adding Custom Pages to Content Tree/Index

    In order to add your HTML files into a content tree and index you should

    1) Prepare content specification for a tree. It is a very simple format that has
    a root tag `help-content` and one or more tags `node` organized into a tree.

    A tag `node` shall have attribute `name` and may have attribute `local` that
    points to the html file.

    ```xml
    <help-content>
        <node name="Lua 5.1 Reference Manual" local="lua/lua_51_reference_manual.html">
            <node name="1 - Introduction" local="lua/1.html"/>
            <node name="2 - The Language" local="lua/2.html"/>
            <node name="2.1 - Lexical Conventions" local="lua/2.1.html"/>
            <node name="2.2 - Values and Types" local="lua/2.2.html">
             <node name="2.2.1 - Coercion" local="lua/2.2.1.html" />
            </node>
        </node>
        ...
    </help-content>
    ```

    2) Prepare a content specification for an index.

    The index is a plain list of `key` tags which has three obligatory attributes: `keyword`, `name` and `local`.

    ```xml
    <help-index>
     <key keyword="Lua 5.1 Reference Manual" name="Lua 5.1 Reference Manual" local="lua/lua_51_reference_manual.html" />
     <key keyword="Lua: 1 - Introduction" name="Lua: 1 - Introduction" local="lua/1.html" />
     <key keyword="Lua: 2 - The Language" name="Lua: 2 - The Language" local="lua/2.html" />
    </help-index>
    ```

    3) Choose a group that will be the parent node for the content and specify
    the XML files using `@importhhc` (content) and `@importhhk` (keywords index)
    attributes.


    #### Customizing Styles

    You can also take the standard styles from the HTML template
    (if you use nuget, the template is located at
     `%USERPROFILE%\.nuget\packages\gehtsoft.build.docgen\__docgen version__\Content\template\html\res`

    You can modify these templates and then overwrite them in `res` folder inside of
    the compiled documentation.

    Don't forget set `external-resources` definition in the template properties to `yes` if you
    want to override styles.

    Alternatively you can override styles using `add-custom-style` option.

    #### Customizing header and footer

    You can add a piece of HTML to: `head` tag, right after `body` tag and right before closing `body` tag.

    Use the following properties of the template `add-to-prolog-head`, `add-to-prolog-body` and
    `add-to-epilog`. The values must be a file name, the content of the specified file
    will be written into corresponding location of the HTML files for articles, groups, and members.

    #### Customizing the frame container

    You can add your own code to the header of the container page using
    `add-to-container-prolog-head` property of the template or
    replace a table cell (including `<td>` tag) that contains
    the header text using `replace-container-prolog-title` property. Both property
    points at a text file that contains a fragment of the HTML to be inserted.

    #### Github Pages Default File

    Default configuration of the html template has following settings:

    - The root group has `index` key and so named `index.html`.
    - The web content files has `web-content.html` name.

    In some environments, e.g. in github pages, it is not possible to
    change the default file. In such environments, the following course of
    actions is suggested:

    - Make the root group key other than `index`, e.g. `main`.
    - Force the naming of the web content file to `index.html` using
      `web-content-file-name` property of the HTML template (set only
      file name, without extension!).
    - If need to support old links directed to `web-content.html`
      add property `web-content-file-name-backward-compatibility`
      and set it to `yes`.



    #### Customize Template

    For the "heavy lifting" customization modify the the template and use your own custom template.
@end

